claude-rag-hook (0.6.1-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * Critical fix: refuse to overwrite ~/.claude.json when it cannot be
    parsed. The v0.6.0 self-install path treated any JSON decode error
    as "empty file" and rewrote the file with only mcpServers, which
    could destroy unrelated user state (other MCP servers, project
    trust entries, OAuth session cache). The fix raises a ParseError
    for non-empty unparseable files; ensure_registered/unregister
    catch it and return False (no change). Affects any user whose
    ~/.claude.json has had a transient malformed state during a hook
    invocation. Recommended upgrade for everyone on v0.6.0.
  * Fix: rename the bundled slash command from `/rag` to `/rag-toggle`
    so it stops shadowing the documented `/rag <text>` retrieval form.
    Existing users who installed v0.6.0's `~/.claude/commands/rag.md`
    can leave it in place (it still works via the legacy filename) or
    delete it; the hook will install `rag-toggle.md` on next run.
  * Fix: never overwrite a user-owned ~/.claude/commands/rag-toggle.md.
    The shipped file now carries a marker comment; we recognise our
    own previous installs via that marker and update them, but leave
    files without the marker alone forever.
  * Fix: tar path-traversal guard in `crh import`. The startswith()
    check let `<dest>-suffix/...` paths slip past the
    `<dest>` prefix. Now uses Path.is_relative_to() which is
    component-aware. tarfile's filter="data" already provides a second
    layer of defence on Python 3.12+; this fixes the explicit guard.
  * Fix: MCP rag_status no longer reports an empty-but-existing
    .claude-rag-index/ directory as "ready". Mirrors the populated
    check the hook already does. An aborted indexing attempt that
    leaves the dir without a chunks.lance subdir is reported as
    state: empty so Claude knows to ask the user to retry.
  * Codex pass found and validated all five fixes.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Sat, 09 May 2026 13:00:00 +0000

claude-rag-hook (0.6.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * New: bundled MCP server (claude-rag-mcp). Companion surface to
    the keyword-triggered hook. Claude Code can now call rag_search,
    rag_status, and rag_list_stores when it judges that retrieval
    would help and the user did not type the keyword (or the keyword
    retrieval returned thin chunks and Claude wants a follow-up
    search). Saves tokens by skipping retrieval when not needed and
    by letting one Claude turn refine its own search rather than
    asking the user to start over. The MCP server is on by default;
    toggle off any time with `crh mcp off`.
  * The MCP server is auto-registered into the per-user
    ~/.claude.json on first hook invocation (idempotent). Same shape
    as `claude mcp add --scope user` would produce. apt postinst
    cannot reliably touch every user's home directory, so the hook
    handles registration when it next runs as the target user. Turn
    off with `crh mcp off` (flips a CLAUDE_RAG_MCP_DISABLED=1 env
    var on the entry; the entry stays present so the toggle is
    reversible from the same place).
  * New: `/rag` slash command. Toggles auto-rag mode from inside
    Claude Code itself. When auto-rag is on, every prompt the user
    submits is treated as if they had typed `rag <prompt>`: the hook
    retrieves relevant chunks and prepends them. Slash commands and
    very short prompts are still passed through. Slash command
    markdown is shipped at /usr/lib/claude-rag-hook/commands/rag.md
    and self-installs to ~/.claude/commands/rag.md on first hook
    run. Equivalent shell command: `crh rag toggle`.
  * New: `crh rag` and `crh mcp` subcommands. Each accepts
    on / off / toggle / status. Persistent state in
    $XDG_STATE_HOME/claude-rag-hook/toggles.json.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Sat, 09 May 2026 12:00:00 +0000

claude-rag-hook (0.5.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Default embedder switched from nomic-ai/nomic-embed-text-v1.5
    to BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5. Quality is essentially the same on
    English retrieval (~62 vs 62.4 MTEB), but the new default has
    33M parameters vs nomic's 137M, produces 384-dim vectors vs
    768-dim, and runs in roughly an order of magnitude less RAM.
    Index size on disk drops by about 2x. The driving constraint
    is RAM-constrained machines (8-16 GB laptops): the previous
    default could OOM during big-repo indexing even with the
    v0.4.1 batch_size fix on top. v0.5.0 fits comfortably on
    those hosts. Power users on big-RAM machines who want nomic's
    longer 8192-token context or a 1-point-higher MTEB score can
    override `embedder.model` in
    ~/.config/claude-rag-hook/config.yaml; see the README for the
    exact config block.
  * Migration is automatic for existing indexes. The retrieval
    path reads each index's recorded embedder from meta.yaml and
    builds a matching embedder per-index, so indexes built before
    the upgrade keep working without intervention. To migrate an
    index to the new default for the RAM/disk benefits, run
    `crh refresh --rebuild` (new flag).
  * `crh status` surfaces an embedder-mismatch hint when an
    existing index was built with a different model than the
    user's currently configured default. The hint is informational
    and points at `crh refresh --rebuild`.
  * `crh index` and `crh refresh` accept a new `--rebuild` flag
    that drops the existing chunks.lance and manifest, then runs
    a full re-embed. Used for migrating between embedders or
    forcing a clean rebuild after corruption.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Fri, 08 May 2026 20:30:00 +0000

claude-rag-hook (0.4.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix: indexer OOM on big repos. fastembed defaults to
    embed(batch_size=256) internally; ONNX runtime allocates
    per-call workspace sized for that batch. On a 768-dim
    transformer that workspace is ~6 GB per call, ratcheting to
    ~12 GB resident over a few calls. A 3815-file monorepo
    indexed from a memory-capped cgroup (eg a konsole tab)
    OOM-killed at file 651/3815. We now pass batch_size=4 to
    the fastembed embed() call, which makes it run mini-batches
    of 4 sequentially internally. Same total throughput
    (compute-bound, not batch-overhead-bound), peak RSS ~1.6 GB.
    New config knob `embedder.fastembed_batch_size` (default 4)
    so power users on big-RAM hosts can crank it back up.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Fri, 08 May 2026 19:15:00 +0000

claude-rag-hook (0.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * New: operator CLI at /usr/bin/crh. Subcommands: status, index,
    refresh, query, forget, ls, tag, untag, doctor, auto on/off,
    refresher run/start/stop/status. Blocking commands (index,
    refresh) show a live progress bar with files-done/total, ETA,
    and elapsed; --json for scripting; --no-watch to suppress the
    bar in CI. The CLI gives you observability that was previously
    only available by tailing log files.
  * New: status `[interrupted]` state. When .progress claims an
    indexing job is active but the pid is dead (kill, OOM, crash,
    power loss), `crh status` reports the partial state and points
    at recovery: `crh refresh` to resume from the manifest, or
    `crh forget` to drop the partial index. Previously the CLI
    would have reported `[ready]` and the user would have a
    half-built index without knowing it.
  * Resilience: indexer now persists the file manifest on a
    throttled checkpoint cadence (every 16 files / 2 seconds),
    atomically (write-tmp + rename + fsync). A killed indexer no
    longer means starting over: `crh refresh` resumes from the
    last checkpoint. Worst-case lost work is the trailing N files
    since the last checkpoint, which the existing size+mtime gate
    handles harmlessly.
  * New: auto-refresh daemon (off by default, opt-in per host AND
    per project). Systemd user unit at
    /usr/lib/systemd/user/claude-rag-hook-refresher.service with
    Nice=19, CPUSchedulingPolicy=idle, IOSchedulingClass=idle, and
    a 50% CPU quota so it yields to foreground work. Per-project
    opt-in is a `.auto-refresh` marker file inside the index dir;
    `crh auto on` drops it. Daemon polls watched projects every
    30 seconds, coalesces changes for 60 seconds of quiet before
    refreshing, and never refreshes the same project more than
    once per 5 minutes. Skips refreshes when loadavg-1m > 1.5 or
    laptop is on battery below 30%.
  * `crh doctor` checks the install end-to-end: hook binary
    present, hook wired into managed-settings.json, fastembed
    importable, lancedb importable, model cache populated,
    embedder daemon socket healthy, no orphan indexers, total
    index disk usage, refresher daemon systemd state.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Fri, 08 May 2026 15:30:00 +0000

claude-rag-hook (0.3.3-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix: indexer now emits throttled per-file progress messages
    during the embed loop, so the .progress file actually reflects
    live state. Before this, files_done was set to 0 at start and
    never updated until the run finished, which meant bare `rag`
    on a real repo (3000+ files) reported "0/3815 files" for the
    entire 30-minute run and looked wedged. The throttle is "every
    16 files OR every 2 seconds, whichever fires first" so the
    progress file isn't rewritten thousands of times per second.

 -- Ramazan Yavuz <yavuzramazan1994@gmail.com>  Fri, 08 May 2026 14:50:00 +0000

claude-rag-hook (0.3.2-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Fix: fastembed model cache no longer lives in /tmp, where it gets
    wiped on every reboot (and intermittently by systemd-tmpfiles
    during uptime), forcing a 4-minute re-download from HuggingFace.
    The hook now points fastembed at /var/cache/claude-rag-hook/models/
    (machine-wide, created by apt postinst, mode 2775 group adm so
    any local user can read and the first user fills the shared
    cache for everyone). Falls back to ~/.cache/claude-rag-hook/models/
    if /var/cache is unwritable (rare; e.g. read-only host).
  * Fix: when the on-disk model cache exists but the .onnx file is
    missing (the /tmp purge case, or any interrupted download),
    fastembed used to throw NoSuchFile and the indexer would
    persist a "failed" .progress that required manual intervention.
    The hook now detects this state, wipes the broken model dir,
    and lets fastembed re-download cleanly. Plus a self-heal retry
    on any TextEmbedding construction error.
  * Install: apt postinst now best-effort pre-fetches the default
    embedding model (~80MB ONNX) into /var/cache so the first
    `rag <q>` is fast on a fresh install. Pre-fetch is silent on
    failure (network down, fastembed not installed, etc.); the
    runtime auto-recovery path covers all the same cases on demand.
  * Install: apt purge now removes /var/cache/claude-rag-hook/. A
    plain `apt remove` leaves the cache so a reinstall is fast.

