claude-rag-hook (0.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * Major redesign: integration tool, not CLI.
  * Drop the user-facing CLI entirely (no more index/query/ls/rm/config/
    daemon/install/uninstall/version subcommands; no /usr/bin/claude-rag-hook
    on $PATH). The package now ships a Claude Code hook and the small
    pieces it needs, all under /usr/lib/claude-rag-hook/. End users
    install the .deb and use Claude Code normally; nothing on the
    command line for them to learn.
  * Wire into Claude Code automatically. The apt postinst merges the
    hook entry into /etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json (the
    documented system-wide settings file Claude Code reads with the
    highest precedence for every user on the machine). Every Claude
    Code session, current and future user, picks up the hook on next
    start without per-user setup.
  * Auto-index on first 'rag:' in a project folder. The hook
    fork-detaches a background indexer for the nearest project root
    (the ancestor with .git, pyproject.toml, package.json, Cargo.toml,
    go.mod, etc.); the user's current prompt passes through to Claude
    unchanged so they get an answer right now. The next 'rag:' uses
    the populated index. Subsequent 'rag:' turns trigger an
    incremental refresh in the background, throttled to 5 minutes.
  * Auto-index safety rails: hard refusal on $HOME and direct
    children, /, /etc, /var, /tmp, /usr, /opt, /root, /boot, /sys,
    /proc, /dev. Project-marker required (a .git or similar) within
    six ancestors of cwd. Size cap: 20k files / 500 MB; bypass via
    CLAUDE_RAG_HOOK_BYPASS_SIZE_CAP=1.
  * Fix settings.json schema. The previous 0.1.0 entry used a fake
    "name" field at the top level of each matcher; Claude Code's real
    schema is {"hooks":[{"type":"command","command":"..."}]} nested
    inside the matcher, no top-level name. uninstall now finds our
    entry by command-equality on the absolute hook path.
  * Drop the <context></context> wrapper from hook output. Per Claude
    Code's documented contract, plain stdout from a UserPromptSubmit
    hook is appended to the prompt as a system reminder; tag wrapping
    is unnecessary.
  * Postinst is idempotent and merges into existing managed-settings.json
    (other admins / other tools' entries are preserved). Postrm
    removes only the entry whose command matches our hook path.

